From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 03:38:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: sleep: space T8132's return windows 10 s
 apart

Three J604 deep sleeps that stopped in the same step reset 78, 78 and
84 s after their RTC alarm, so windows 5 s apart (65/70/75) cannot be
told apart. Most of that spread is the boot chain after the reset,
which the next boot can measure, but 5 s leaves no margin.

Put every step of the way back 10 s from the next: the stub 60,
__cpu_suspend_exit() 70 and 80, the DAIF restore 90,
apple_sleep_return() 100 and 110, device resume 120 (unchanged) and
the SError unmask 130. WD2's alarm is 32 bits of a 24 MHz count
(apple_wdt caps it at U32_MAX / rate), so nothing can go past 178 s,
which is why the steps are not wider.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/soc/apple/sleep.c | 26 ++++++++++++++++----------
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/sleep.c b/drivers/soc/apple/sleep.c
index acf83a480c..66ba926658 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/sleep.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/sleep.c
@@ -191,19 +191,26 @@ static_assert(AURA_S2R_CHECKED_SIZE + sizeof(struct aura_s2r_slc) == AURA_S2R_SL
 #define WDT_SLEEP_SECS		5
 /* Device suspend and resume can take a while, and nothing else recovers a hang in them. */
 #define WDT_SUSPEND_SECS	120
+/*
+ * T8132's way back, each step its own WD2 window, 10 s apart, so the time from the
+ * wake to a reset names the step: stub 60, __cpu_suspend_exit() 70 and 80, the DAIF
+ * restore 90, apple_sleep_return() 100 and 110, device resume 120, SError unmasked
+ * 130, an exception before VBAR is restored 145/160/175. WD2's alarm is 32 bits of a
+ * 24 MHz count, so nothing past 178 s.
+ */
 /*
  * The way back has its own WD2 windows, each a different length, so the time from the
  * wake to a reset says which step stopped: the resume stub's 60 s, then these, then
  * WDT_SUSPEND_SECS over device resume. Nothing else survives that reset.
  */
-#define WDT_RETURN_SECS		80
-#define WDT_REWAKE_SECS		100
+#define WDT_RETURN_SECS		100
+#define WDT_REWAKE_SECS		110
 /* Last on the way back: SError unmasked again, before device resume. */
-#define WDT_UNMASK_SECS		140
+#define WDT_UNMASK_SECS		130
 /* Inside the generic return, T8132: see t8132_return_mark(). */
-#define WDT_EXIT_FIRST_SECS	65
-#define WDT_EXIT_LAST_SECS	70
-#define WDT_DAIF_SECS		75
+#define WDT_EXIT_FIRST_SECS	70
+#define WDT_EXIT_LAST_SECS	80
+#define WDT_DAIF_SECS		90
 /* ISR_EL1.A: an SError pending */
 #define SLEEP_ISR_A		BIT(8)
 
@@ -2481,10 +2488,9 @@ static void apple_sleep_lower_io(struct apple_sleep *s)
 }
 
 /*
- * T8132's way back stops between the resume stub's 60 s window and apple_sleep_return()'s
- * 80 s. These split the generic return in between, so a reset names the step: 60 the
- * resume assembly itself, 65 the first line of __cpu_suspend_exit(), 70 its last, 75 the
- * DAIF restore. Plain writes, no call: it runs before the idmap is gone.
+ * T8132's way back stops between the resume stub's 60 s window and apple_sleep_return().
+ * These split the generic return in between (see WDT_SUSPEND_SECS for the ladder).
+ * Plain writes, no call: it runs before the idmap is gone.
  */
 static notrace void t8132_return_mark(enum arm64_suspend_mark mark)
 {
-- 
